Q1:
The system should identify a user's region based on their IP adreess.
Ipv4 addressed are written as four numbers serrated y periods, like "127.10.20.30". Each number is called an octer. Based on the first octer, address fall into one the five regions:
1. 0.0.0.0 -127.255.255.255
2.128.0.0.0 - 191.255.255.255
3.192.0.0.0 - 223.255.255.255
4.224.0.0.0 - 239.255.255.255
5.240.0.0.0 - 255.255.255.255
you are given a list of IP addresssed. For each one, check of it is valid, if it is validated, return the region index(1 through 5). If it is not validated, return -1
Input: ["127.0.0.1", "192.168.1.1", "256.256.256.256", "244.178.44.111"]
Output: [1, 3, -1, 4]

Q2:
Given an array taskMemory of n positive integers representing the memory required for each task,
an array taskType of n positive integers representing task types,
and an integer maxMemory, find the minimum time required to process all tasks.

Each task takes 1 unit of time.
The server can process at most two tasks in parallel only if:
	•	They are of the same type, and
	•	Together they require no more than maxMemory units of memory.

⸻
Example
Suppose
n = 4
taskMemory = [7, 2, 3, 9]
taskType   = [1, 2, 1, 3]
maxMemory  = 10

One efficient schedule is shown below:
Task Pair       Task 1      Task 2      Task Type       Memory Requirement      Within Max Memory
1               0           2           Same            7 + 3 = 10              Yes
2               1           3           Different       9 + 2 = 11              No

Tasks 0 and 2 are processed concurrently because:
	•	They are of the same type (type = 1).
	•	Their total memory usage (7 + 3 = 10) is within the maxMemory limit.

Tasks 1 and 3 must be processed separately because:
	•	They are of different types, or
	•	Their combined memory exceeds maxMemory.

Hence, the minimum time required to process all tasks is 3 units.

More examples:
Example1:
taskMemory = [7, 2, 3, 9]
taskType   = [1, 2, 1, 3]
maxMemory  = 10
✅ Output: 3
Explanation:
	•	Type 1: tasks (7, 3) → can pair (7 + 3 = 10 ≤ 10) → 1 unit
	•	Type 2: (2) → alone → 1 unit
	•	Type 3: (9) → alone → 1 unit

Test Case 2 — All same type, all fit perfectly
Input:
taskMemory = [4, 6, 3, 7]
taskType   = [1, 1, 1, 1]
maxMemory  = 10
Explanation:
Sort memory per type → [3, 4, 6, 7]
	•	(3 + 7 = 10) → 1 unit
	•	(4 + 6 = 10) → 1 unit
✅ Output: 2

 Test Case 3 — No two tasks can pair
 taskMemory = [8, 9, 10]
 taskType   = [1, 1, 1]
 maxMemory  = 10
 Explanation:
 	•	8 + 9 > 10, 8 + 10 > 10, 9 + 10 > 10 → all must run separately
 ✅ Output: 3

Test Case 4 — Multiple types with mixed pairings
taskMemory = [2, 5, 4, 6, 3, 7]
taskType   = [1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 2]
maxMemory  = 8
Explanation:
	•	Type 1: [2, 4, 5] → (2+5=7 ≤8) + (4 alone) → 2 units
	•	Type 2: [3, 6, 7] → (3+6=9>8) → all must run alone → 3 units

✅ Total = 2 + 3 = 5
✅ Output: 5

Test Case 5 — Single task
taskMemory = [9]
taskType   = [1]
maxMemory  = 10
✅ Output: 1

🧪 Test Case 6 — All different types
taskMemory = [2, 3, 4, 5]
taskType   = [1, 2, 3, 4]
maxMemory  = 10
Explanation:
Each task has a unique type → none can run in parallel.
✅ Output: 4

Test Case 7 — Pairing within same type, uneven count
taskMemory = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
taskType   = [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]
maxMemory  = 6

Explanation: Sorted = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
	•	(1 + 5 = 6) → 1 unit
	•	(2 + 4 = 6) → 1 unit
	•	(3 alone) → 1 unit
✅ Output: 3

Test Case 8 — Tight memory constraint
taskMemory = [4, 4, 4, 4]
taskType   = [1, 1, 1, 1]
maxMemory  = 7
Explanation: 4 + 4 = 8 > 7 → no pairs possible → all separate
✅ Output: 4

Test Case 9 — Large groups with same type
 taskMemory = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]
 taskType   = [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]
 maxMemory  = 9
Explanation: Sorted = [1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8]
Pairs:
	•	(1+8=9), (2+7=9), (3+6=9), (4+5=9) → 4 pairs → 4 units
✅ Output: 4

Test Case 10 — Mix of solo and paired same type
taskMemory = [8, 3, 9, 4, 5, 6]
taskType   = [1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 3]
maxMemory  = 10
Explanation:
	•	Type 1 → [3, 8] → (3+8=11>10) → 2 units
	•	Type 2 → [4,5,9] → (4+5=9 ≤10) + (9 alone) → 2 units
	•	Type 3 → [6] → 1 unit
✅ Total = 5
✅ Output: 5